<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>9.6.2: Choosing the Best Method to Solve an Equation</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02419</md:content-id>
    <md:title>9.6.2: Choosing the Best Method to Solve an Equation</md:title>
    <md:uuid>28661166-8cfe-4c13-be30-52cc3ed2d6e9</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Activity (10 minutes)</title>
<para>In this activity, students encounter equations that are challenging to solve using the methods they have learned, motivating students to seek a more efficient method.</para>
<title>Launch</title>
<para>Arrange students in groups of two. Ask partners to choose the same equation. Give students quiet time to solve the equation and then time to discuss their solutions and strategy. If they finish solving their chosen equation, ask them to choose another one to solve. Leave a few minutes for a whole-class discussion.</para>
<para>Provide access to calculators for numerical computations.</para>
<div class="os-raise-extrasupport">
  <note>
    <para><title>Support for English Language Learners</title></para>
    <para><title>MLR 8 Discussion Supports: Speaking, Representing</title></para>
  </note>
  <div class="os-raise-extrasupport-body">

    <para>Use this routine to support small-group discussion. Provide the class with the following sentence frames to help them discuss their solution strategies with their partner: “First I _____ because. . .”, “I tried _____ and what happened was . . .”, “How did you get . . . ?”, and “Why did you . . . ?” Encourage students to challenge each other when they disagree. This will help students clarify their reasoning when solving quadratic equations.</para>
    <para class="os-raise-text-italicize">Design Principle(s): Support sense-making</para>
</div>
</div>
<div class="os-raise-extrasupport">
  <note>
    <para><title>Support for Students with Disabilities</title></para>
  <para><title>Engagement: Develop Effort and Persistence</title></para>
   </note>
  <div class="os-raise-extrasupport-body">
    <para>Connect a new concept to one with which students have experienced success. Activate prior knowledge by reminding students that they have already successfully completed tasks by both factoring and completing the square. Invite them to brainstorm what might be important to consider when selecting a method to use.</para>
    <para class="os-raise-text-italicize">Supports accessibility for: Social-emotional skills; Conceptual processing</para>
  </div>
</div>
<title>Student Activity</title>
<para>Write down your answer for how to solve each equation. Find the two solutions by rewriting it in factored form or by completing the square. Be prepared to explain your choice of method.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item><emphasis>Select two</emphasis> solutions for \(x^2−2x−1.25=0\).</item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent">
  <item> 2.5 </item>
  <item> 3 </item>
  <item> -1 </item>
  <item> -0.5 </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 2.5, -0.5</para>
<para>Select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> Completing the square method:</para>
<para>\(\begin{array}{rcl} x^{2}-2 x-1.25 &amp;=&amp;0 \\
  x^{2}-2 x &amp;=&amp;1.25 \\
  x^{2}-2 x+\left(\frac{-2}{2}\right)^{2} &amp;=&amp;1.25+\left(\frac{-2}{2}\right)^{2} \\
  x^{2}-2 x+1 &amp;=&amp;1.25+1 \\
  x^{2}-2 x+1 &amp;=&amp;2.25 \\
  (x-1)^{2} &amp;=&amp;2.25 \\
  \sqrt{(x-1)^{2}} &amp;=&amp;\sqrt{2.25} \\
  x-1 &amp;=&amp;\pm \sqrt{2.25} \\
  x &amp;=&amp;1 \pm \sqrt{2.25} \\
  x &amp;=&amp;1 \pm 1.5 \\
  x &amp;=&amp;1+1.5 \\
  x &amp;=&amp;1-1.5 \\
  x &amp;=&amp;2.5 \\
  x &amp;=&amp;-0.5 \\ x&amp;=&amp;2.5 \text { and } x=-0.5
  \end{array}\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item><emphasis>Select two</emphasis> solutions for \(5x^2+9x−44=0\).</item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent">
  <item> 2.2 </item>
  <item> -20 </item>
  <item> -4 </item>
  <item> 11 </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 2.2, -4</para>
<para>Select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> Completing the square method:</para>
<para>\(\begin{array}{rcl} 5 x^{2}+9 x-44 &amp;=&amp;0 \\
  5 x^{2}+9 x &amp;=&amp;44 \\
  \frac{5 x^{2}+9 x}{5} &amp;=&amp;\frac{44}{5} \\
  x^{2}+\frac{9}{5} x+\left(\frac{1}{2} \cdot \frac{9}{5}\right)^{2} &amp;=&amp;\frac{44}{5}+\left(\frac{1}{2} \cdot \frac{9}{5}\right)^{2} \\
  x^{2}+\frac{9}{5} x+\frac{81}{100} &amp;=&amp;\frac{44}{5}+\frac{81}{100} \\
  x^{2}+\frac{9}{5} x+\frac{81}{100} &amp;=&amp;\frac{961}{100} \\
  \left(x+\frac{9}{10}\right)^{2} &amp;=&amp;\frac{961}{100} \\
  \sqrt{\left(x+\frac{9}{10}\right)^{2}} &amp;=&amp;\sqrt{\frac{961}{100}} \\
  x+\frac{9}{10} &amp;=&amp;\pm \frac{31}{10} \\
  x &amp;=&amp;-\frac{9}{10} \pm \frac{31}{10} \\
  x &amp;=&amp;-\frac{9}{10}+\frac{31}{10} \\
  x &amp;=&amp;-\frac{9}{10}-\frac{31}{10} \\
  x &amp;=&amp;\frac{22}{10}=2.2\\
  x&amp;=&amp;-\frac{40}{10}=-4 \\
  x&amp;=&amp;2.2 \text { and } x=-4
  \end{array}\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item><emphasis>Select two </emphasis>solutions for \(x^2+1.25x=0.375\).</item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent">
  <item> 0.75 </item>
  <item> 0.25 </item>
  <item> -1.5 </item>
  <item> 2.5 </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 0.25, -1.5</para>
<para>Select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> Completing the square method:</para>
<para>\(\begin{aligned} x^{2}+1.25 x &amp;=0.375 \\ x^{2}+1.25 x+((0.5)(1.25))^{2} &amp;=0.375+((0.5)(1.25)){ }^{2} \\ x^{2}+1.25 x+0.390625 &amp;=0.375+0.390625 \\ (x+0.625)^{2} &amp;=0.765625 \\ \sqrt{(x+0.625)^{2}} &amp;=\sqrt{0.765625} \\ x+0.625 &amp;=\pm 0.875 \\ x &amp;=-0.625 \pm 0.875 \\ x &amp;=-0.625+0.875 \\ x &amp;=-0.625-0.875 \\ x &amp;=0.25 \\ x &amp;=-1.5 \\ x=0.25 &amp; \text { and } x=-1.5 \end{aligned}\) </para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item><emphasis>Select two</emphasis> solutions for \(4x^2−28x+29=0\).</item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent">
  <item> \(-7+ \sqrt{2}\) </item>
  <item>\(7- \sqrt{2}\) </item>
  <item> \(\frac{7}{2}+\sqrt{5}\) </item>
  <item> \(\frac{7}{2}- \sqrt{5}\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(\frac{7}{2}+ \sqrt{5}\), \(\frac{7}{2}- \sqrt{5}\) </para>
<para>Select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> Completing the square method:</para>
<para>\(\begin{array}{rcl} 4 x^{2}-28 x+29 &amp;=&amp;0 \\
  \frac{4 x^{2}-28 x+29}{4} &amp;=&amp;0 \\
  x^{2}-7 x+\frac{29}{4} &amp;=&amp;0 \\
  x^{2}-7 x &amp;=&amp;-\frac{29}{4} \\
  x^{2}-7 x+\left(\frac{7}{2}\right)^{2} &amp;=&amp;-\frac{29}{4}+\left(\frac{7}{2}\right)^{2} \\
  x^{2}-7 x+\frac{49}{4} &amp;=&amp;\frac{20}{4} \\
  x^{2}-7 x+\frac{49}{4} &amp;=&amp;5 \\
  \left(x-\frac{7}{2}\right)^{2} &amp;=&amp;5 \\ \sqrt{\left(x-\frac{7}{2}\right)^{2}} &amp;=&amp;\sqrt{5} \\
  x-\frac{7}{2} &amp;=&amp;\pm \sqrt{5} \\ x&amp;=&amp;\frac{7}{2}\pm\sqrt{5} \\ x&amp;=&amp;\frac{7}{2}+\sqrt{5} \\ x&amp;=&amp;\frac{7}{2}-\sqrt{5} \\
  x&amp;=&amp;\frac{7}{2}+\sqrt{5} \text { and } x=\frac{7}{2}-\sqrt{5}
  \end{array}
  \) </para>
<title>Activity Synthesis</title>
<para>Consider arranging students who solved the same equation in groups of two to three to discuss their strategies and then displaying the correct solutions for all to see.</para>
<para>Invite students to share their reflections on the solving process. Discuss questions such as:</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent">
  <item> "What method did you choose and why?" </item>
  <item> "Did you find yourself choosing one method and then switching to another? If so, what prompted you to do that?" </item>
  <item> "Did you run into any challenges when rewriting the equation (or completing the square)? What were some of the challenges?" </item>
</list>
<para>Acknowledge that all of these equations are cumbersome to solve by either rewriting in factored form or completing the square. The last equation cannot be written in factored form (with rational coefficients), so completing the square is the only way to go. Tell students they are about to learn a formula that gives the solutions to any quadratic equation.</para>
<title>9.6.2: Self Check </title>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold"><emphasis>After the activity, students will answer the following question to check their understanding of the concepts explored in the activity.</emphasis></para>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold">QUESTION:</para>
<para>Use completing the square to solve \(2x^2+10x+8=0\).</para>
<table class="os-raise-textheavytable">
  <thead>
    <row>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">ANSWERS</entry>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">FEEDBACK</entry>
    </row>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry><para>\(x= \frac{25}{4}\)</para></entry>
      <entry><para>Incorrect. Let's try again a different way: This is the value that completed the square. The equation then needs to be solved. The answer is \(x=-4, x=-1\).</para></entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry><para>\(x= 1, x =4\)</para></entry>
      <entry><para>Incorrect. Let's try again a different way: Make sure to subtract the \(c\) from both sides before completing the square, not add the positive value. The answer is \(x=-4, x=-1\).</para></entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry><para>\(x=-4, x=-1\)</para></entry>
      <entry><para>That's correct! Check yourself: After taking the square root of both sides, the equation becomes \(x=-\frac{5}{2} \pm \frac{3}{2}\).</para></entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry><para>\(x=-5 \pm \sqrt{17}\)</para></entry>
      <entry><para>Incorrect. Let's try again a different way: Divide all terms by the coefficient, 2, before completing the square, not just the first term. The answer is \(x=-4, x=-1\).</para></entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>
<title>9.6.2: Additional Resources</title>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold"><emphasis>The following content is available to students who would like more support based on their experience with the self check. Students will not automatically have access to this content, so you may wish to share it with those who could benefit from it.</emphasis></para>
<title>Solve by Using Perfect Squares and Completing the Square </title>
<para>Solve \(2x^2+5x+2=0\) by completing the square.</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Divide all terms by the leading coefficient. </para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Move the constant to the right side of the equation. </para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Complete the square and add to each side: \((\frac{5}{2} \times \frac{1}{2})^2=\frac{25}{16}\). </para>
<para><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - Simplify.  </para>
<para><emphasis>Step 5</emphasis> - Take the square root of each side. </para>
<para><emphasis>Step 6</emphasis> - Write two equations and solve. </para>
<title>Try It: Solve by Using Perfect Squares and Completing the Square</title>
<para>Solve \(4x^2+36x+37=0\).</para>
<para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
<title>Solution</title>
<para>Here is how to solve using completing the square:</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Divide all terms by the leading coefficient. </para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Move the constant to the right side of the equation. </para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Complete the square and add to each side: \((9 \times \frac{1}{2})^2=\frac{81}{4}\). </para>
<para><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - Simplify.  </para>
<para><emphasis>Step 5</emphasis> - Take the square root of each side. </para>
<para><emphasis>Step 6</emphasis> - Write two equations and solve. </para>
</section>
</content>
